1
00:00:00,818 --> 00:00:04,426
On day 121, the hill wants your speaker.

2
00:00:04,848 --> 00:00:06,931
The woman is still waiting on the radio.

3
00:00:06,931 --> 00:00:09,409
Down in the bunker, the three of you sit

4
00:00:09,409 --> 00:00:11,750
on the bench with the lantern turned low,

5
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:14,474
and nobody wants to be the first to speak.

6
00:00:14,474 --> 00:00:16,034
Your name is Patch.

7
00:00:16,393 --> 00:00:17,836
A hundred and twenty days ago,

8
00:00:17,836 --> 00:00:20,533
you were waiting for a bus.

9
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:23,733
Since then, you have lived under a

10
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:26,322
bus depot with Nora, who used to clean it,

11
00:00:26,322 --> 00:00:29,028
and Wes, who used to live on a roof.

12
00:00:29,028 --> 00:00:30,917
Last month, you filled the yard

13
00:00:30,917 --> 00:00:33,109
with thousands of the dead and

14
00:00:33,109 --> 00:00:34,774
got locked out for your trouble.

15
00:00:34,774 --> 00:00:36,259
You walked home in the rain

16
00:00:36,259 --> 00:00:38,187
and climbed a rope over the wall.

17
00:00:38,187 --> 00:00:40,130
Now the tail of that same horde is

18
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:42,148
climbing toward the people on the hill,

19
00:00:42,148 --> 00:00:44,057
and the only thing loud enough to

20
00:00:44,057 --> 00:00:46,605
turn it around is bolted to your canopy.

21
00:00:47,075 --> 00:00:49,005
"If we call them," Nora whispers,

22
00:00:49,005 --> 00:00:50,391
"where do they go?"

23
00:00:50,989 --> 00:00:51,869
You all know.

24
00:00:51,869 --> 00:00:53,001
The yard is full.

25
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:54,780
They will stop at the gate.

26
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:55,677
In the street.

27
00:00:55,677 --> 00:00:56,935
Under the sign.

28
00:00:57,244 --> 00:00:59,107
Right where the rope came down.

29
00:00:59,107 --> 00:01:00,446
"She put the whole city

30
00:01:00,446 --> 00:01:02,489
on our ceiling," Nora says.

31
00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:03,796
"Now she wants the rest

32
00:01:03,796 --> 00:01:05,402
of it on our doorstep."

33
00:01:05,824 --> 00:01:07,030
"She turned the truck

34
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:08,409
around for me," you say.

35
00:01:08,409 --> 00:01:09,986
You think about day 90.

36
00:01:09,986 --> 00:01:10,804
The street.

37
00:01:10,804 --> 00:01:12,303
The tail of the river

38
00:01:12,303 --> 00:01:13,839
turning to follow you.

39
00:01:13,839 --> 00:01:15,436
A cab door swinging open

40
00:01:15,436 --> 00:01:17,627
before you even knew it was there.

41
00:01:17,627 --> 00:01:20,028
You have owed people things before.

42
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:21,600
Never anything this big,

43
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:24,324
and never to someone whose name you don't know.

44
00:01:24,889 --> 00:01:26,624
Wes raises one hand.

45
00:01:26,624 --> 00:01:28,176
"I vote yes.

46
00:01:28,176 --> 00:01:28,874
Quietly.

47
00:01:28,874 --> 00:01:31,345
In case anyone's listening."

48
00:01:32,103 --> 00:01:33,349
Nora looks at the chart

49
00:01:33,349 --> 00:01:34,855
on the wall for a long time.

50
00:01:34,855 --> 00:01:35,674
The days.

51
00:01:36,127 --> 00:01:37,199
The walkers gone.

52
00:01:37,199 --> 00:01:38,151
The people met.

53
00:01:38,151 --> 00:01:40,038
Then she picks up the radio.

54
00:01:40,038 --> 00:01:41,918
"We'll do it," she says.

55
00:01:41,918 --> 00:01:43,689
"On one condition."

56
00:01:44,223 --> 00:01:45,818
"Name it," says the woman.

57
00:01:45,818 --> 00:01:47,256
"Food and water.

58
00:01:47,256 --> 00:01:48,137
Every week.

59
00:01:48,137 --> 00:01:48,944
Delivered."

60
00:01:48,944 --> 00:01:51,985
"Your street is about to be full of them."

61
00:01:51,985 --> 00:01:54,317
"Then you'll have to be clever about it.

62
00:01:54,317 --> 00:01:56,124
Patch says you're very clever."

63
00:01:56,124 --> 00:01:58,115
A long pause on the channel.

64
00:01:58,730 --> 00:01:59,256
"Fine."

65
00:01:59,256 --> 00:02:02,271
"And one more thing," says Nora.

66
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:03,544
"Your name."

67
00:02:03,544 --> 00:02:05,609
This pause is longer.

68
00:02:06,272 --> 00:02:08,403
"Ask me again when you can see my face,"

69
00:02:08,403 --> 00:02:11,725
says the woman, and the radio goes quiet.

70
00:02:11,725 --> 00:02:15,213
Day 122 begins at the control box.

71
00:02:15,213 --> 00:02:16,412
Wes opens the lid.

72
00:02:16,412 --> 00:02:17,610
The little battery

73
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:19,424
light glows fully green.

74
00:02:19,424 --> 00:02:20,713
For a whole month,

75
00:02:20,713 --> 00:02:23,827
the sun has been quietly filling it up again,

76
00:02:23,827 --> 00:02:25,483
while nobody was asking it to.

77
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:27,020
Nora stands beside

78
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:28,524
him with her arms folded.

79
00:02:28,524 --> 00:02:30,579
"I spent two weeks trying to

80
00:02:30,579 --> 00:02:32,469
shut that thing up," she says.

81
00:02:32,469 --> 00:02:33,903
"I know," says Wes.

82
00:02:34,276 --> 00:02:35,318
"It's on the list of

83
00:02:35,318 --> 00:02:36,567
things I'm sorry about."

84
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:37,961
He flips the switch.

85
00:02:38,512 --> 00:02:39,382
Over the yard,

86
00:02:39,382 --> 00:02:41,702
over the street and over the rooftops,

87
00:02:41,702 --> 00:02:44,687
a voice you know rolls out across the town.

88
00:02:44,687 --> 00:02:46,806
"Passengers for Platform 1."

89
00:02:46,806 --> 00:02:50,135
From the gantry, you watch the hill road.

90
00:02:50,135 --> 00:02:51,720
The dark line that has been

91
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:54,660
crawling uphill all night slows down.

92
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:55,251
Stops.

93
00:02:55,251 --> 00:02:56,191
Then,

94
00:02:56,191 --> 00:02:59,750
slowly, like the needle of a compass, it turns.

95
00:03:00,189 --> 00:03:01,868
All day, they come back down.

96
00:03:01,868 --> 00:03:04,079
At the split, they take the low road.

97
00:03:04,079 --> 00:03:06,244
Then they pour around the last corner

98
00:03:06,244 --> 00:03:09,684
into your street, the way water fills a ditch.

99
00:03:09,684 --> 00:03:10,625
By afternoon,

100
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:13,357
the street is full from wall to wall.

101
00:03:13,357 --> 00:03:14,851
They stand under the sign.

102
00:03:14,851 --> 00:03:16,590
Around the lamppost.

103
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:18,166
Against the parked cars.

104
00:03:18,166 --> 00:03:20,979
All of them looking up at the canopy,

105
00:03:20,979 --> 00:03:22,185
waiting for the bus.

106
00:03:22,185 --> 00:03:24,913
Above them, the sign sways in the wind.

107
00:03:24,913 --> 00:03:28,246
For the first time, it has an audience.

108
00:03:28,246 --> 00:03:31,090
At sunset, Wes switches the speaker off.

109
00:03:31,496 --> 00:03:33,754
The stragglers keep arriving after dark,

110
00:03:33,754 --> 00:03:36,476
walking toward the last thing they heard.

111
00:03:36,476 --> 00:03:38,367
In the morning, there is a figure at

112
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:40,849
the gate with both hands flat against it,

113
00:03:40,849 --> 00:03:42,834
like a man waiting to be buzzed in.

114
00:03:43,385 --> 00:03:44,666
An olive vest.

115
00:03:44,666 --> 00:03:47,396
"He's back," Wes whispers.

116
00:03:47,396 --> 00:03:48,988
"He must have missed us."

117
00:03:48,988 --> 00:03:51,888
Then you notice something strange.

118
00:03:51,888 --> 00:03:53,373
The gate isn't rattling.

119
00:03:53,373 --> 00:03:55,037
The yard is pushing it out.

120
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:56,778
The street is pushing it in.

121
00:03:56,778 --> 00:03:58,372
For the first time in months,

122
00:03:58,372 --> 00:04:00,584
the gate stands perfectly straight.

123
00:04:01,119 --> 00:04:02,200
"That's not how it's

124
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:04,068
supposed to work," says Nora.

125
00:04:04,555 --> 00:04:06,567
"Nothing is," says Wes.

126
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:08,103
On the radio,

127
00:04:08,103 --> 00:04:09,920
the woman's voice is calm again.

128
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:11,486
"They've left the hill.

129
00:04:11,861 --> 00:04:12,969
All of them."

130
00:04:12,969 --> 00:04:13,736
A pause.

131
00:04:13,736 --> 00:04:14,674
"Thank you."

132
00:04:14,674 --> 00:04:17,175
You have never heard her say it before.

133
00:04:17,175 --> 00:04:19,423
Neither, you suspect, has she.

134
00:04:19,423 --> 00:04:20,945
That night, at the hour,

135
00:04:20,945 --> 00:04:24,076
Nora blinks the flashlight twice from the gantry.

136
00:04:24,076 --> 00:04:27,383
And far up the hill, on top of the tallest tank,

137
00:04:27,383 --> 00:04:28,759
a small light blinks back.

138
00:04:28,759 --> 00:04:31,604
Somebody up there has learned the code.

139
00:04:31,604 --> 00:04:33,033
From day 123,

140
00:04:33,033 --> 00:04:35,482
the depot is an island.

141
00:04:35,482 --> 00:04:37,665
Nobody can reach the wall.

142
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,206
Nobody can reach the gate.

143
00:04:40,206 --> 00:04:43,123
The sign hangs over a sea of heads,

144
00:04:43,123 --> 00:04:44,704
and the place where the rope

145
00:04:44,704 --> 00:04:47,190
came down is just more shoulders.

146
00:04:47,190 --> 00:04:50,253
In the bunker, the footsteps on the

147
00:04:50,253 --> 00:04:52,854
ceiling never stop now, not even at night.

148
00:04:53,469 --> 00:04:56,092
Thousands of feet shifting on concrete,

149
00:04:56,092 --> 00:04:57,507
slow as weather.

150
00:04:57,961 --> 00:04:59,008
Wes says it sounds

151
00:04:59,008 --> 00:05:00,795
like living under a beach.

152
00:05:01,394 --> 00:05:04,305
Nora says she has never been to a beach.

153
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:05,968
Wes promises to take her,

154
00:05:05,968 --> 00:05:09,126
when all this is over, which is the kind of

155
00:05:09,126 --> 00:05:11,886
promise that only works down here.

156
00:05:11,886 --> 00:05:14,880
On day 124, Nora lays everything you own

157
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:17,448
across the bench and does the arithmetic

158
00:05:17,448 --> 00:05:20,992
in a whisper, the way she did on day 35.

159
00:05:20,992 --> 00:05:22,041
Twenty days of food if

160
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:23,888
you eat like normal people.

161
00:05:24,326 --> 00:05:25,264
Twenty-five if you

162
00:05:25,264 --> 00:05:27,609
eat like careful people.

163
00:05:27,609 --> 00:05:29,822
Thirty if you eat like people

164
00:05:29,822 --> 00:05:31,662
who are about to start arguing.

165
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:33,301
Two and a half jugs of water.

166
00:05:33,301 --> 00:05:34,604
You choose careful.

167
00:05:34,604 --> 00:05:36,455
You know the way by now.

168
00:05:36,828 --> 00:05:37,933
On day 125,

169
00:05:37,933 --> 00:05:41,378
the three of you share the last apple.

170
00:05:41,378 --> 00:05:43,310
You pass it around and suck

171
00:05:43,310 --> 00:05:45,184
each bite until it gives up.

172
00:05:45,184 --> 00:05:47,369
It takes most of the afternoon.

173
00:05:47,904 --> 00:05:51,335
It is the best afternoon of the week.

174
00:05:51,335 --> 00:05:53,375
On day 126, it rains.

175
00:05:53,375 --> 00:05:55,327
Nora stands at the end of the

176
00:05:55,327 --> 00:05:57,303
gantry and watches the water.

177
00:05:57,303 --> 00:06:00,516
It runs along the edge of the canopy roof,

178
00:06:00,516 --> 00:06:02,359
through the long metal gutter,

179
00:06:02,359 --> 00:06:05,809
and pours out of a pipe into the packed yard below,

180
00:06:05,809 --> 00:06:07,837
where it does nobody any good.

181
00:06:08,323 --> 00:06:10,473
"Nine years," she says.

182
00:06:10,473 --> 00:06:12,072
"Once a year,

183
00:06:12,072 --> 00:06:14,720
up that ladder, scraping that gutter.

184
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:16,162
I hated every rung."

185
00:06:16,162 --> 00:06:20,461
You climb over the gantry rail onto the canopy roof,

186
00:06:20,461 --> 00:06:22,334
the way you did on day 80.

187
00:06:22,334 --> 00:06:24,043
Hands and knees.

188
00:06:24,043 --> 00:06:25,511
Spreading your weight.

189
00:06:25,511 --> 00:06:28,017
Every time the thin metal flexes,

190
00:06:28,017 --> 00:06:28,688
it booms,

191
00:06:28,688 --> 00:06:32,743
and under the canopy, the coach rocks on its springs.

192
00:06:32,743 --> 00:06:35,442
At the corner, you pull the top of

193
00:06:35,442 --> 00:06:37,322
the drainpipe out of the gutter and

194
00:06:37,322 --> 00:06:40,680
wedge an empty water jug under the spout.

195
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,047
It fills in an hour.

196
00:06:43,047 --> 00:06:45,907
By day 128, there are two full

197
00:06:45,907 --> 00:06:48,895
jugs at the bottom of the bunker stairs,

198
00:06:48,895 --> 00:06:51,360
and the rain is still falling.

199
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:53,349
Wes tastes it and declares it

200
00:06:53,349 --> 00:06:55,586
the finest vintage of the season.

201
00:06:56,121 --> 00:06:59,015
It tastes of metal roof and pigeons.

202
00:06:59,015 --> 00:07:00,723
Nobody complains.

203
00:07:01,642 --> 00:07:04,283
Nora looks at the jugs for a long time.

204
00:07:04,283 --> 00:07:06,009
"Every rung," she says.

205
00:07:06,009 --> 00:07:07,343
"Every single one.

206
00:07:07,343 --> 00:07:08,904
It was for this."

207
00:07:09,663 --> 00:07:12,520
Water, it turns out, falls from the sky.

208
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:13,796
Food doesn't.

209
00:07:14,315 --> 00:07:16,353
On the night of day 129,

210
00:07:16,353 --> 00:07:19,227
the woman's voice comes on the radio.

211
00:07:19,227 --> 00:07:21,047
"Look across the street."

212
00:07:21,694 --> 00:07:23,720
Opposite the far end of the gantry,

213
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:26,326
above a little shop with a torn awning,

214
00:07:26,326 --> 00:07:28,296
there is a window on the first floor.

215
00:07:28,845 --> 00:07:30,825
In it, a light blinks once.

216
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:33,502
She came on foot, from the back alley.

217
00:07:33,502 --> 00:07:34,922
Her truck coasted in with

218
00:07:34,922 --> 00:07:36,816
its engine off and is parked

219
00:07:36,816 --> 00:07:39,205
three streets away, out of sight.

220
00:07:39,205 --> 00:07:41,296
On day 130, she leans out of the

221
00:07:41,296 --> 00:07:43,668
window with a coil of thin cord.

222
00:07:43,668 --> 00:07:46,054
There is a heavy bolt tied to its end.

223
00:07:46,054 --> 00:07:48,402
Her first throw falls short.

224
00:07:48,402 --> 00:07:50,473
The bolt drops into the crowd,

225
00:07:50,473 --> 00:07:52,429
and a hundred faces turn toward it.

226
00:07:52,429 --> 00:07:54,100
She pulls it back,

227
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:57,898
slowly, very slowly, through a forest of legs.

228
00:07:57,898 --> 00:08:00,825
Her second throw clears the gantry rail.

229
00:08:01,294 --> 00:08:03,176
Wes catches it against his chest,

230
00:08:03,176 --> 00:08:05,060
and doesn't make a sound,

231
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:08,404
and is extremely proud of himself for the rest of the day.

232
00:08:08,825 --> 00:08:09,622
You pull.

233
00:08:09,622 --> 00:08:12,314
Behind the cord comes a rope.

234
00:08:12,655 --> 00:08:14,360
You tie it to the rail.

235
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:17,471
On her side, she ties it to a radiator.

236
00:08:17,471 --> 00:08:20,981
Now a line runs across the street,

237
00:08:20,981 --> 00:08:24,023
high over a few thousand heads.

238
00:08:24,023 --> 00:08:25,343
On day 131,

239
00:08:25,343 --> 00:08:28,719
the first sack comes across, hanging from a hook.

240
00:08:28,719 --> 00:08:30,622
The line sags in the middle.

241
00:08:30,622 --> 00:08:32,797
Every face below follows it,

242
00:08:32,797 --> 00:08:35,571
like the crowd at a very slow tennis match.

243
00:08:35,946 --> 00:08:36,544
Tins.

244
00:08:36,544 --> 00:08:37,540
Crackers.

245
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:39,235
Ration packs.

246
00:08:39,722 --> 00:08:41,231
Then a full water jug,

247
00:08:41,231 --> 00:08:44,867
so heavy that the line dips low over the crowd.

248
00:08:44,867 --> 00:08:47,316
Green fingers brush the bottom of it.

249
00:08:47,316 --> 00:08:49,439
You and Wes pull faster than

250
00:08:49,439 --> 00:08:51,635
you have ever pulled anything.

251
00:08:51,635 --> 00:08:56,810
From day 132 to day 135,

252
00:08:56,810 --> 00:08:57,081
the

253
00:08:57,081 --> 00:08:59,049
deliveries become a routine.

254
00:08:59,049 --> 00:09:00,080
Twice a week,

255
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:03,249
after dark, a light blinks in the window opposite.

256
00:09:03,622 --> 00:09:05,492
Then the sacks come across,

257
00:09:05,492 --> 00:09:08,218
one at a time, never two at once.

258
00:09:08,218 --> 00:09:10,663
The woman always comes alone.

259
00:09:10,663 --> 00:09:12,418
When she leaves,

260
00:09:12,418 --> 00:09:14,377
the engine starts first time,

261
00:09:14,377 --> 00:09:17,538
three streets away, and she is gone before the

262
00:09:17,538 --> 00:09:19,769
slowest of them has turned its head.

263
00:09:20,144 --> 00:09:21,681
The rest of the hill stays

264
00:09:21,681 --> 00:09:22,933
behind its fence and,

265
00:09:22,933 --> 00:09:27,762
for the first time in months, sleeps all night.

266
00:09:27,762 --> 00:09:30,713
On day 133, Nora stands at the rail and

267
00:09:30,713 --> 00:09:32,843
sees the woman's face for the first time,

268
00:09:33,153 --> 00:09:35,814
in the window, in the light of a lantern.

269
00:09:36,269 --> 00:09:37,208
The goggles.

270
00:09:37,208 --> 00:09:39,026
The long grey scarf.

271
00:09:39,738 --> 00:09:41,120
"Goggles," Nora says.

272
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:42,801
"You weren't joking."

273
00:09:43,658 --> 00:09:44,275
That night,

274
00:09:44,275 --> 00:09:46,520
the sacks hold batteries, a can of oil,

275
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:50,029
more blue bandages, and a small tin of white paint.

276
00:09:50,029 --> 00:09:51,631
"For your sign," the

277
00:09:51,631 --> 00:09:53,287
woman says over the radio.

278
00:09:53,287 --> 00:09:54,691
"Yours is running."

279
00:09:55,323 --> 00:09:58,420
Wes immediately wants to change

280
00:09:58,420 --> 00:10:00,873
THREE to FOUR, to see what happens.

281
00:10:00,873 --> 00:10:03,110
"We're not four," says Nora.

282
00:10:03,437 --> 00:10:05,002
"We could be," says Wes.

283
00:10:05,002 --> 00:10:07,207
"She's just across the road."

284
00:10:07,597 --> 00:10:10,411
Nora puts the paint on the rack, unopened.

285
00:10:10,411 --> 00:10:15,959
From day 136 to day 140,

286
00:10:15,959 --> 00:10:17,528
the nights get colder.

287
00:10:17,528 --> 00:10:20,309
Your breath shows on the gantry.

288
00:10:20,309 --> 00:10:22,757
The metal rail stings your hands.

289
00:10:22,757 --> 00:10:24,798
In the yard and in the street,

290
00:10:24,798 --> 00:10:26,647
the dead stand a little closer together,

291
00:10:26,647 --> 00:10:29,213
though they couldn't possibly feel it.

292
00:10:29,213 --> 00:10:30,083
In the coach,

293
00:10:30,083 --> 00:10:33,674
frost creeps across the windows from the corners,

294
00:10:33,674 --> 00:10:36,214
and eleven faces watch it come.

295
00:10:36,214 --> 00:10:37,626
Every delivery night,

296
00:10:37,626 --> 00:10:41,110
the woman stays at her window after the last sack,

297
00:10:41,110 --> 00:10:44,134
and you stay at the end of the gantry.

298
00:10:44,134 --> 00:10:44,996
Two radios.

299
00:10:44,996 --> 00:10:46,848
Twelve meters of air.

300
00:10:47,398 --> 00:10:49,355
A few thousand people in between,

301
00:10:49,355 --> 00:10:51,036
none of them listening.

302
00:10:51,571 --> 00:10:54,733
On day 137, you ask her why she

303
00:10:54,733 --> 00:10:58,127
never goes into places with one way out.

304
00:10:58,127 --> 00:11:00,604
She is quiet for a while.

305
00:11:00,604 --> 00:11:02,528
"On day four," she says,

306
00:11:03,127 --> 00:11:05,494
"I was in a supermarket with forty people.

307
00:11:05,494 --> 00:11:07,111
It had one way out."

308
00:11:07,111 --> 00:11:09,832
Then: "That's all you get."

309
00:11:10,672 --> 00:11:11,736
On day 139,

310
00:11:11,736 --> 00:11:14,998
Nora takes the radio and asks again.

311
00:11:14,998 --> 00:11:16,992
"Can I have your name yet?"

312
00:11:16,992 --> 00:11:19,839
"You can see my face," the woman admits.

313
00:11:19,839 --> 00:11:22,306
"But I'm on the wrong side of the street."

314
00:11:22,873 --> 00:11:24,591
At dawn on day 141,

315
00:11:24,591 --> 00:11:29,030
the radio crackles, and it is not delivery night.

316
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:29,601
"Depot."

317
00:11:29,601 --> 00:11:32,047
The woman's voice is very flat.

318
00:11:32,047 --> 00:11:33,159
"The truck won't start.

319
00:11:33,159 --> 00:11:34,958
The cold got into the battery."

320
00:11:35,557 --> 00:11:36,778
Then you hear it,

321
00:11:36,778 --> 00:11:38,543
faint, over the rooftops.

322
00:11:38,543 --> 00:11:41,310
A grinding cough, three streets away.

323
00:11:41,310 --> 00:11:43,200
The starter motor.

324
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:43,994
Again.

325
00:11:43,994 --> 00:11:45,241
And again.

326
00:11:45,241 --> 00:11:48,418
In the street below the gantry,

327
00:11:48,418 --> 00:11:49,436
heads turn.

328
00:11:49,436 --> 00:11:51,942
"Stop," Nora whispers into the radio.

329
00:11:51,942 --> 00:11:53,471
"Stop turning the key."

330
00:11:54,103 --> 00:11:55,307
The grinding stops.

331
00:11:55,307 --> 00:11:57,233
But a slow movement has already

332
00:11:57,233 --> 00:11:59,596
started at the edge of the crowd.

333
00:11:59,596 --> 00:12:00,817
Part of it peels away,

334
00:12:00,817 --> 00:12:03,430
down the side road, toward the sound.

335
00:12:03,430 --> 00:12:04,105
Then more.

336
00:12:04,105 --> 00:12:05,209
They don't hurry.

337
00:12:05,209 --> 00:12:06,429
They never do.

338
00:12:06,884 --> 00:12:08,629
But there are hundreds of them,

339
00:12:08,629 --> 00:12:10,074
and only one of her.

340
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:11,781
From the end of the gantry,

341
00:12:11,781 --> 00:12:14,372
you can see over the rooftops toward the side road.

342
00:12:14,372 --> 00:12:16,406
You can see where they are going.

343
00:12:16,406 --> 00:12:17,364
She can't.

344
00:12:17,706 --> 00:12:19,568
"Not the hill road," you whisper.

345
00:12:19,568 --> 00:12:21,904
"They're coming down the side road.

346
00:12:22,214 --> 00:12:22,913
Go left.

347
00:12:22,913 --> 00:12:25,087
The alley behind the shops."

348
00:12:25,087 --> 00:12:27,159
For an hour, you watch the crowd

349
00:12:27,159 --> 00:12:28,685
you called down from the hill

350
00:12:28,685 --> 00:12:30,580
walk away from your street,

351
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:32,700
toward the woman who asked you to call it.

352
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:33,429
Then,

353
00:12:33,429 --> 00:12:36,597
in the window opposite, a light blinks once.

354
00:12:36,597 --> 00:12:37,970
She made it.

355
00:12:37,970 --> 00:12:40,537
Then another sound, from behind the shops.

356
00:12:40,537 --> 00:12:41,611
A dustbin,

357
00:12:41,611 --> 00:12:44,617
tipping over in the back alley.

358
00:12:44,617 --> 00:12:48,020
Day 142 dawns with the woman at the window opposite,

359
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:49,016
and the sound of

360
00:12:49,016 --> 00:12:51,348
fists on a door somewhere behind her.

361
00:12:51,348 --> 00:12:53,153
The dead found the truck.

362
00:12:53,817 --> 00:12:55,228
Then they found the alley.

363
00:12:55,228 --> 00:12:56,992
Then they found the stairs.

364
00:12:57,528 --> 00:12:59,016
She has pushed a wardrobe

365
00:12:59,016 --> 00:13:00,675
against the door at the top.

366
00:13:00,675 --> 00:13:01,810
It moves a little

367
00:13:01,810 --> 00:13:03,384
more every time they lean.

368
00:13:03,384 --> 00:13:05,158
The only thing left in that

369
00:13:05,158 --> 00:13:08,129
room that goes anywhere is the line.

370
00:13:08,129 --> 00:13:10,716
"I don't go into places with one

371
00:13:10,716 --> 00:13:13,171
way out," she says on the radio.

372
00:13:13,171 --> 00:13:15,514
"You're already in one," you say.

373
00:13:15,514 --> 00:13:17,184
"Come into ours.

374
00:13:17,184 --> 00:13:18,879
At least it has us in it."

375
00:13:18,879 --> 00:13:20,891
There is a long pause.

376
00:13:20,891 --> 00:13:23,406
Behind her, the wardrobe shifts again.

377
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,380
Nora takes the radio.

378
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:27,311
"You told me to ask again

379
00:13:27,311 --> 00:13:29,297
when I could see your face."

380
00:13:29,297 --> 00:13:30,661
"Not now, Nora."

381
00:13:31,308 --> 00:13:32,866
"Fine," says Nora.

382
00:13:32,866 --> 00:13:33,807
"After."

383
00:13:34,438 --> 00:13:36,799
She ties a loop of cord under her arms,

384
00:13:36,799 --> 00:13:38,148
clips it to the hook,

385
00:13:38,148 --> 00:13:40,260
and climbs onto the windowsill.

386
00:13:40,779 --> 00:13:41,901
She comes across the

387
00:13:41,901 --> 00:13:43,504
street the way the sacks did.

388
00:13:43,504 --> 00:13:44,880
Hanging from the hook,

389
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:46,400
both hands on the line,

390
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:50,197
her goggles pushed up, her scarf knotted at her chest.

391
00:13:50,197 --> 00:13:52,340
You and Wes haul on the pull cord,

392
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:53,367
hand over hand.

393
00:13:53,367 --> 00:13:55,594
She is heavier than any sack.

394
00:13:55,594 --> 00:13:58,623
The line sags lower than it ever has.

395
00:13:58,623 --> 00:13:59,977
Halfway across,

396
00:13:59,977 --> 00:14:03,748
it dips down toward the crowd, and a few thousand

397
00:14:03,748 --> 00:14:05,784
faces tilt up to watch her come.

398
00:14:06,996 --> 00:14:07,550
Arms rise,

399
00:14:07,550 --> 00:14:10,179
slowly, like a field of wheat in the wind.

400
00:14:10,717 --> 00:14:12,526
A green hand brushes her foot.

401
00:14:12,526 --> 00:14:13,643
Then another.

402
00:14:14,325 --> 00:14:16,729
One of them comes out of an olive sleeve.

403
00:14:16,729 --> 00:14:18,545
Of course it does.

404
00:14:18,545 --> 00:14:20,546
"Pull," Nora whispers,

405
00:14:20,546 --> 00:14:23,201
and holds the flashlight on her,

406
00:14:23,201 --> 00:14:26,679
steady, the way she has held it every night

407
00:14:26,679 --> 00:14:28,161
for a hundred and forty-two days.

408
00:14:28,717 --> 00:14:29,535
You pull.

409
00:14:29,535 --> 00:14:31,863
The line rises again.

410
00:14:31,863 --> 00:14:33,289
The hook reaches the rail,

411
00:14:33,289 --> 00:14:35,234
and Nora grabs the woman's arm and

412
00:14:35,234 --> 00:14:37,510
drags her over onto the gantry.

413
00:14:37,510 --> 00:14:38,363
Behind her,

414
00:14:38,363 --> 00:14:41,874
in the window across the street, the door gives way.

415
00:14:42,376 --> 00:14:43,973
Green shapes fill the room.

416
00:14:43,973 --> 00:14:46,384
One of them walks into the radiator.

417
00:14:46,774 --> 00:14:47,715
The knot slips.

418
00:14:47,715 --> 00:14:50,773
And the line drops into the crowd, gone.

419
00:14:50,773 --> 00:14:52,509
The woman sits on the metal

420
00:14:52,509 --> 00:14:55,045
floor of the gantry and looks back at

421
00:14:55,045 --> 00:14:57,202
the empty window for a long time.

422
00:14:57,202 --> 00:14:59,074
"Well," she says, at last.

423
00:14:59,074 --> 00:15:01,048
"That's one way out."

424
00:15:01,048 --> 00:15:04,262
From day 143 to day 149,

425
00:15:04,262 --> 00:15:07,990
there are four of you in a bunker for two.

426
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:09,918
Wes offers her the stairs.

427
00:15:10,276 --> 00:15:11,859
She looks at the steel door at

428
00:15:11,859 --> 00:15:13,639
the top of them and says no,

429
00:15:14,044 --> 00:15:15,741
thank you, very politely,

430
00:15:15,741 --> 00:15:19,349
like someone refusing a seat on a sinking ship.

431
00:15:19,349 --> 00:15:20,943
So Wes keeps the stairs.

432
00:15:20,943 --> 00:15:22,713
The woman sleeps sitting up

433
00:15:22,713 --> 00:15:23,765
against the wall,

434
00:15:23,765 --> 00:15:27,195
facing the steel door, because it is the only door.

435
00:15:27,195 --> 00:15:28,348
On the first morning,

436
00:15:28,348 --> 00:15:30,063
she reorganizes the racks.

437
00:15:30,404 --> 00:15:31,684
On the second morning,

438
00:15:31,684 --> 00:15:32,853
Nora puts them back.

439
00:15:32,853 --> 00:15:34,191
On the third morning,

440
00:15:34,191 --> 00:15:36,431
nobody touches the racks, and that is how a

441
00:15:36,431 --> 00:15:38,777
peace treaty gets signed down here.

442
00:15:38,777 --> 00:15:40,705
She doesn't complain about the dark,

443
00:15:40,705 --> 00:15:43,093
or the ceiling, or the footsteps.

444
00:15:43,093 --> 00:15:44,575
She just listens to them,

445
00:15:44,575 --> 00:15:46,703
the way you did on day one,

446
00:15:46,703 --> 00:15:48,734
as if she is learning a language.

447
00:15:48,734 --> 00:15:50,980
Wes asks whether she counts as

448
00:15:50,980 --> 00:15:53,231
people met twice, now that she's indoors.

449
00:15:53,231 --> 00:15:54,388
Nobody answers him.

450
00:15:54,388 --> 00:15:55,086
At sunset,

451
00:15:55,086 --> 00:15:58,184
the hill's radio speaks with a new voice,

452
00:15:58,184 --> 00:16:00,265
one of the dozen, nervous and young.

453
00:16:00,265 --> 00:16:01,622
Without the truck,

454
00:16:01,622 --> 00:16:03,201
nobody up there can herd.

455
00:16:03,201 --> 00:16:04,112
But nothing is

456
00:16:04,112 --> 00:16:06,208
climbing the hill road anymore.

457
00:16:06,208 --> 00:16:08,116
Everything that walks in this

458
00:16:08,116 --> 00:16:11,070
town is standing around your depot.

459
00:16:11,070 --> 00:16:13,193
For once, it is the hill

460
00:16:13,193 --> 00:16:15,708
that has a quiet ceiling.

461
00:16:15,708 --> 00:16:18,584
On day 147, Wes opens the tin of

462
00:16:18,584 --> 00:16:21,103
white paint and looks at Nora hopefully.

463
00:16:21,798 --> 00:16:23,074
"Not until she tells

464
00:16:23,074 --> 00:16:24,905
us her name," says Nora.

465
00:16:25,327 --> 00:16:27,441
"Then leave it at three," says the woman,

466
00:16:27,441 --> 00:16:28,915
without looking up.

467
00:16:29,257 --> 00:16:31,138
The tin goes back on the rack.

468
00:16:31,138 --> 00:16:33,043
Food for about a month,

469
00:16:33,043 --> 00:16:34,527
if you are careful.

470
00:16:34,527 --> 00:16:36,904
Water, as long as it rains.

471
00:16:37,582 --> 00:16:38,314
On day 150,

472
00:16:38,314 --> 00:16:41,877
the woman climbs to the gantry with you at dawn.

473
00:16:42,235 --> 00:16:43,895
There is frost on the rail.

474
00:16:43,895 --> 00:16:44,843
For a long time,

475
00:16:44,843 --> 00:16:46,523
she looks down at the yard.

476
00:16:46,523 --> 00:16:47,609
Thousands of them,

477
00:16:47,609 --> 00:16:50,164
packed from the gate to the canopy.

478
00:16:50,164 --> 00:16:51,697
And in the middle of them,

479
00:16:51,697 --> 00:16:53,644
under the canopy, the coach.

480
00:16:54,193 --> 00:16:57,345
Its folding door, shut since day 55.

481
00:16:57,814 --> 00:16:59,786
Its luggage hold, empty.

482
00:16:59,786 --> 00:17:02,858
Its windows, furred with frost.

483
00:17:02,858 --> 00:17:05,197
"Nora," she says quietly.

484
00:17:05,650 --> 00:17:07,004
"This is a bus depot."

485
00:17:07,004 --> 00:17:07,519
"I know.

486
00:17:07,519 --> 00:17:08,722
I cleaned it."

487
00:17:09,256 --> 00:17:10,818
"And that is a bus."

488
00:17:10,818 --> 00:17:12,462
"Its battery died on

489
00:17:12,462 --> 00:17:13,989
day three," says Nora.

490
00:17:13,989 --> 00:17:15,871
The woman looks up at the solar

491
00:17:15,871 --> 00:17:17,934
panel on the canopy roof,

492
00:17:17,934 --> 00:17:20,022
sitting directly above the coach,

493
00:17:20,022 --> 00:17:22,977
the panel you cleaned one strip at a time.

494
00:17:22,977 --> 00:17:25,243
"Batteries can be charged," she says.

495
00:17:25,243 --> 00:17:26,793
"If that coach could drive,

496
00:17:26,793 --> 00:17:30,256
I could take your whole yard out of that gate behind it.

497
00:17:30,256 --> 00:17:31,280
And your street.

498
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:32,983
And never bring them back."

499
00:17:32,983 --> 00:17:35,471
Wes looks from the coach to the woman.

500
00:17:35,471 --> 00:17:36,536
"Who drives?"

501
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:38,695
The woman pulls her goggles

502
00:17:38,695 --> 00:17:39,820
down over her eyes,

503
00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:42,912
for the first time since you have known her.

504
00:17:42,912 --> 00:17:44,377
"I always drive."

505
00:17:44,913 --> 00:17:47,536
You look down through the coach windows.

506
00:17:47,536 --> 00:17:48,796
Eleven passengers.

507
00:17:49,252 --> 00:17:51,238
The one at the top of the aisle,

508
00:17:51,238 --> 00:17:53,594
as always, is the night driver,

509
00:17:53,594 --> 00:17:56,268
in his blue depot vest with its two pockets.

510
00:17:56,804 --> 00:17:58,997
"It would need its keys," you say.

511
00:17:58,997 --> 00:18:01,618
Nora doesn't answer for a while.

512
00:18:01,618 --> 00:18:04,110
When she does, it is barely a whisper.

513
00:18:04,517 --> 00:18:06,618
"They're in his pocket."

514
00:18:06,618 --> 00:18:08,045
A hundred and fifty days.

515
00:18:08,045 --> 00:18:09,591
The same orange bandage.

516
00:18:09,591 --> 00:18:11,870
Nora is holding the flashlight.

517
00:18:12,276 --> 00:18:14,712
You are holding the orange bag.

518
00:18:14,712 --> 00:18:16,407
Tomorrow is day 151.

519
00:18:16,407 --> 00:18:19,591
There are four of you in a bunker for two,

520
00:18:19,591 --> 00:18:20,952
with the dead on both

521
00:18:20,952 --> 00:18:23,921
sides of the gate and frost on the rail.

522
00:18:23,921 --> 00:18:25,579
The woman who never goes into

523
00:18:25,579 --> 00:18:27,496
places with one way out has

524
00:18:27,496 --> 00:18:29,634
finally found the way out of yours.

525
00:18:29,634 --> 00:18:30,927
And the key to it is

526
00:18:30,927 --> 00:18:32,079
in a dead man's pocket.

527
00:18:32,079 --> 00:18:34,482
Would you have turned the speaker on?

528
00:18:34,482 --> 00:18:35,655
Which decision would

529
00:18:35,655 --> 00:18:37,162
you have made differently?

530
00:18:37,162 --> 00:18:38,848
Tell us in the comments,

531
00:18:38,848 --> 00:18:41,227
and subscribe to Last Safe Door,

532
00:18:41,227 --> 00:18:43,646
so you're here for day 151.
